# Cover Letter Generation Instructions

<!--
Copy this file to instructions.md (already git-ignored) and fill in your
own details. This file is read by the Planner, Generator, and Reviewer
agents to guide their output — the structure below mirrors what those
agents expect, with placeholders instead of real personal info.
-->

These are the fixed rules for generating [Your Name]'s cover letters. Follow this structure exactly every time, filling in only the bracketed/company-specific sections.

## Formatting

- **Header** (top):
  - Name: **[Your Full Name]** — 15pt, Times New Roman — **centered**
  - Contact line: [City, State] | [Phone] | [Email] | [LinkedIn URL] | [GitHub URL] | [Portfolio URL] — 11pt, Times New Roman — **centered**
  - LinkedIn, GitHub, and Portfolio should be hyperlinked but styled in **black text**, not the default blue hyperlink color
  - Everything from the date onward (date, role name, salutation, body, closing) is **left-aligned**
- **All body text**: 11pt, Times New Roman
- **Top left, above the salutation**:
  - Date (the date the letter is generated/requested)
  - Name of the role, on the line underneath the date

## Delivery

- Once the letter is created, save it as a .docx file and return it directly to [Your Name] in the conversation so they can review and upload it themselves.
- Do **not** upload or save the file to Google Drive.

## Structure

### 1. Salutation
```
Dear Recruiter,
```

### 2. Opening paragraph (fixed — do not alter wording)
```
My name is [Your Name], and I am writing to express my interest in the [Role Name] at [Firm Name]. I am currently [studying/working as] [your field/role] at [your school/company], with a strong focus on [your key strengths/focus areas]. I believe the following skills make me a strong fit for this program.
```

### 3. Three skill paragraphs
- Each formatted as an indented bullet point
- Each has a **bolded header/title** (e.g., **[Skill Area]:**) followed by the paragraph content
- Content should be pulled from [Your Name]'s past cover letters and background ([list your key experiences — internships, projects, research, coursework]) and tailored to match the specific role's requirements
- Refer to [Your Name]'s past example letters for tone, phrasing style, and level of detail

### 4. Company-specific paragraph (fixed template — fill in brackets only)
```
As for my interest in [Firm Name], it is deeply rooted in the firm's ability to [specific detail about the firm — e.g., a strategy, technology, market position, or approach]. [One to two sentences expanding on this detail]. I am enthusiastic about the possibility of bringing my skills to [Firm Name] and look forward to discussing how my background and experience would allow me to contribute to the [Team Name] team.
```

**Research requirements for this paragraph:**
- Pull specific, concrete details about the firm — not generic language like "innovation" or "excellence"
- Prioritize details about: the firm's specific business strategy, market position or technology, and either **firm values** or **[any group-specific programs/initiatives relevant to you]** — at least one of these two should be included
- Search the firm's official site, press releases, and recent news for this content

### 5. Closing
```
Sincerely,

[Your Name]
```

## Source Material
- Reference [Your Name]'s past cover letters (stored in this project) for tone, sentence rhythm, and how they typically frame their experience within the three skill paragraphs
- Do not invent experience — only draw from what appears in [Your Name]'s actual background/past letters/resume
